#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116153} :activity/id #uuid "b08a34fb-70a3-5629-9b6a-f9a78ef3fef7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050962 :topic/id #uuid "75aea66f-2cbf-5c78-849b-d3faa857b9b4" :topic/math-academy-id 1585} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116505 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=f(-x)?$\n\n![](images/51/517dbe715d1f1e79756e6e7bd0d733cc5894f425ac02b2ac1bde2c2a33e86c8d.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(-x).$\n\nFor example, let's take the point $(3,4)$. The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means $f(3)=4.$ Then, if $x={\\color{red}-3},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f\\left(-({\\color{red}-3})\\right) = f(3) = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis means the point $({\\color{red}3},4)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $({\\color{red}-3},4)$ that lies on $y=f(-x).$\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x),$ there will be a point on the graph of $y=f(-x)$ with the *same* $y$-value and the *opposite* $x$-value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(-x)[/math]} \\\\ (-1,2) & (1,2) \\\\ (0,-1) & (0,-1) \\\\ (2,0) & (-2,0) \\\\ (3,4) & (-3,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of $y = f(-x){:}$\n\n![](images/3a/3ae62ef6496a598d27e485990ab0d3b3cb6f23211de4a015d54614db463bb131.png)\n\nLooking back at the graph, we see that every point on the graph of $y=f(-x)$ is the **reflection across the $y$-axis** of a point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nSo, the graph of $y=f(-x)$ can be obtained by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the $y$-axis. This result holds in general." :tutorial/id #uuid "333ac18d-4b7d-5c9b-b443-fb3a705515ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d346ae0a-17ad-5edb-b7f6-751cd8a3e2e7" :step/math-academy-id 18023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115091 :question/id #uuid "8bbf173b-820a-5b50-b40f-72e4ef09267a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4977"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ecd0f74-19c3-5770-b376-4d945320f06f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540091}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of a Horizontally Reflected Function"} :step/id #uuid "d6940736-3c57-5ee2-a4a0-e708da2a4b61" :step/math-academy-id 18028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115092 :question/id #uuid "5b49540d-4bcd-5ba9-b258-d6ad9828f0e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4981"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6912eee-a9ca-5a57-8564-d276afc29711" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540090}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Transformed Function"} :step/id #uuid "b05811d8-83b0-566f-8237-c7f9fd933b5f" :step/math-academy-id 18030}] :activity/title "Horizontal Reflections of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115419}]}
